{
  "title": "A shortest significant ciphertext lag fixes harmonics but confuses periodic plaintext with cyclic columns",
  "program": "J",
  "on_path": true,
  "hard": true,
  "statement": "On 18 planted cyclic-column ciphertexts from two new books, maximum exact-word recurrence over lags 2–160 recovers the true period in 9/18 and otherwise selects a multiple; an exploratory shortest-significant-lag rule recovers all 18 and rejects no 18 random-column historical-text cases. But with random columns and a repeated ten-character plaintext, all nine tests reject and eight report lag 10: periodic plaintext itself can mimic the same evidence. The shortest-lag rule was chosen after inspecting the first experiment. Neither test recovers the plaintext or key.",
  "check": "Register any period-search rule and threshold before independent new books, keys and structured controls. Calibrate arbitrary periodic plaintext, repeated passages, column schedules with gaps or change points, ciphertext collisions, transcription noise, random and nonperiodic but correlated sources, and held-out lag scores. Report uncertainty and false alarms by independent text rather than counting keys as texts. Develop and calibrate the requested uncertainty-aware unknown-column decoder and compare with known-column oracles before applying it to manuscripts; a detected period is not a decipherment.",
  "generator": "Use the pinned Hermes P.III CSV (SHA-256 fe3f24a46dbc6b655eab8dbbd75addf84ee4a7dbd387ad802af75d02edfe5b4d). Normalize the pinned it_villani and fr_rose works via vs.refcorpus.blocks_of, concatenate only letters present in the table alphabet (spaces and out-of-alphabet characters removed), and take characters 4,000–11,999. The books were not included in the earlier five-book Latin recovery pilot; the selections are Italian and French rather than Latin. For each period 10, 24 or 131 and seed 207, 411 or 917, sample that many distinct table rows. For cyclic mode use the rows in sequence; for random mode sample row indices independently and uniformly. Output the table word for the plaintext character and selected row. The evaluator knows the planted period and plaintext; the search does not.",
  "search": "Take the first 4,000 ciphertext words. For lags 2–160 calculate exact word-equality rate between tokens separated by that lag. Baseline reports the lag with maximum rate, breaking ties toward smaller lags. To calibrate maximum selection, compare all lag rates to the maxima from 49 independent global shuffles of the word sequence; p=(1+#shuffle maxima >= observed)/50. The exploratory revised rule reports the smallest lag with this maximum-adjusted p <= .05. Report the selected peak rate on the following disjoint 4,000 ciphertext tokens, but do not claim this is a separate significance test. NumPy 2.5.4 default_rng is used for shuffles; seeds and complete results are included.",
  "controls": "For each period and seed generate an independent ten-character random plaintext motif using seed+3000, repeat it to 8,000 characters, encrypt with independently sampled random columns, and run the identical search. This artificial control shows plaintext can produce cyclic-looking exact-word recurrence without a cyclic column schedule; it is not a representative historical text. It was added after inspecting the first experiment. Distinct row selections applied to the same text are not independent books.",
  "limits": "The search’s no-period null destroys all sequential plaintext structure, so rejection cannot identify a cipher schedule. The shortest-significant rule and adversarial control are exploratory and require new independent confirmation. Exact-word equality ignores OCR/transcription noise and may fail on variable spellings; selected row counts are treated as true cycle length although equivalent table rows could reduce the observable period. The synthetic motif is not a natural-language null. No Bayesian partition decoder, recovery of unknown keys, period uncertainty interval, or manuscript test is provided; P-J-026 remains open.",
